​🚀Java Full-Stack Development — Practicing Variables, Data Types & Output Statements
​INTRODUCTION
​Excited to share my latest hands-on coding practice from my Full-Stack Developer training journey with FullStack Experts Academy! Today, I applied variable declarations, primitive and non-primitive data types, and console printing techniques in Java.
​WHAT I WORKED ON
​Wrote and tested a complete Java program (UserDetails.java) to manage user profile information.
​Implemented various data types (String, int, boolean, char, double, and long) to store real-world data points.
​Utilized string concatenation (+) inside System.out.println() to display formatted output to the console.
​KEY LEARNINGS & TECHNOLOGIES
​Variable Declaration & Initialization: Storing temporary data in named memory containers using the syntax DataType VariableName = value;.
​Data Types in Action:
​String for text data (wrapped in double quotes).
​int and long for numeric data (using the L suffix for large long literals).
​boolean for true/false states.
​char for single characters (wrapped in single quotes).
​double for decimal values.
​Java Code Implementation:
public class UserDetails {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Variable declarations
        String name = "Hero";
        int age = 21;
        boolean isEmployed = true;
        char gender = 'M';
        double salary = 120000.00;
        long mobileNo = 9876543210L; // 'L' suffix required for long literals

        // Printing variables to verify values
        System.out.println("Name: " + name);
        System.out.println("Age: " + age);
        System.out.println("Is Employed: " + isEmployed);
        System.out.println("Gender: " + gender);
        System.out.println("Salary: " + salary);
        System.out.println("Mobile No: " + mobileNo);
    }
}
KEY TAKEAWAYS
​Gained strong familiarity with mapping real-world variables to precise Java data types.
​Practiced clean syntax habits, such as avoiding double quotes around variable names during output operations.
​Maintained consistent daily habits through hands-on IDE execution.
